.page mount 8 mount a filesystem or list the mounts

# SYNOPSIS
*mount*
*mount* [*-t* _TYPE_] [*-o* _OPTIONS_] [*-r*] _SOURCE_ _TARGET_

# DESCRIPTION
Without arguments `mount` lists the mounted filesystems as `SOURCE on TARGET type TYPE`, from `/proc/mounts`. With a source and a target it asks the kernel to mount _SOURCE_ (a device such as `/dev/sdb1`, the first partition of a USB stick, or `/dev/vda2`) on the directory _TARGET_. Only root may mount. The type is `fat32` for a `/dev/...` source unless given with `-t` (`fat32`, `vfat` and `fat16` are the same driver; `tmpfs` needs no device).

The Human Village is mounted by the kernel itself: the one volume labelled HUMANVILLAGE, on an internal disk or on a USB stick, appears on `/HumanVillage` at boot or when the stick is plugged in. Nothing else is ever mounted by itself; a second stick is mounted by hand with this command.

# OPTIONS
@ *-t* _TYPE_
    The filesystem type.
@ *-o* _OPTIONS_
    Comma-separated options for a FAT volume: `ro`, `rw`, `force` (mount a volume that was not unmounted cleanly read-write anyway; its dirty flag then stays; `mount` does not go through the volume first the way the boot-time mount of the Human Village does, see fsck(8)), `sync`, `uid=N`, `gid=N`, `fmask=OCT`, `dmask=OCT`, `umask=OCT`, `userdirs` (a top-level directory named like a user belongs to that user), and `tzoffset=MINUTES`.
@ *-r*
    Read-only.

FAT stores the time of a file as the local time of the machine that wrote it, without saying which. `tzoffset=MINUTES` says how many minutes east of UTC that clock is (`-300` for New York in winter, `540` for Tokyo). Unless the option is given, `mount` passes the current offset of its own time zone (see timezone(5)); the kernel applies it when it writes and reads times, so a file saved here at 12:04 shows 12:04 on the computer that reads the stick next.

# EXAMPLES
    mount
    mount -t tmpfs scratch /mnt
    mount /dev/sdb1 /mnt
    mount -o ro /dev/sdb1 /mnt
    mount -o tzoffset=540 /dev/sdb1 /mnt

# EXIT STATUS
0, or 1 if the mount failed (the message includes the kernel's reason).

# SEE ALSO
umount(8), fsck(8), lsblk(1), lsusb(1), dmesg(1), timezone(5)
